LE("regresi-logistik-multinomial",
   [("The baseline-category logit model and its interpretation",
     [r"For a nominal response with \(J\) categories, the model has \(J-1\) logit equations, each comparing one category with the reference category. The exponentiated coefficient is a relative risk ratio (RRR): the factor by which the ratio of the probabilities of category \(j\) and the reference changes for a one-unit increase in the predictor. Comparisons between two non-reference categories come from the difference of their coefficients."]),
    ("The IIA assumption",
     [r"The multinomial logit model assumes independence of irrelevant alternatives (IIA): the ratio of the probabilities of two categories does not depend on what other categories are available. The classic violation is the red bus and blue bus: adding a blue bus should take passengers mainly from the red bus, but an IIA model forces it to take them proportionally from every mode. The Hausman–McFadden test checks this assumption; if it is violated, consider nested logit or multinomial probit."]),
    ("Marginal effects and predicted probabilities",
     [r"The sign of a coefficient in one equation is not always the direction of change in that category's probability, because the probability of the reference category also changes. The marginal effect on the probability of category \(j\) accounts for all equations at once. Presenting predicted probabilities for each category at several predictor values is often easiest for readers to understand."]),
    ("Sample size and rare categories",
     [r"Each equation needs enough events; a rough guide is at least 10 observations per parameter for the rarest category. Very rare categories make the estimates unstable; consider merging categories with similar meaning. Choose a reference category that is reasonably large and easy to interpret."])],
   [(r"For category B against reference A, the income coefficient is 0.3. Interpret it.",
     r"\(e^{0.3}=1.35\): each one-unit increase in income raises the ratio of the probability of choosing B rather than A by 35%."),
    (r"The income coefficient is 0.3 for B (against A) and 0.8 for C (against A). What is the relative risk ratio of C against B?",
     r"\(e^{0.8-0.3}=e^{0.5}=1.65\)."),
    (r"Explain the IIA assumption with an example of transport modes.",
     r"The ratio of the probabilities of choosing a car rather than a red bus must not change even if a blue bus is added. In reality the blue bus takes passengers from the red bus, so IIA is violated."),
    (r"The response has 4 categories and there are 3 predictors. How many parameters are estimated?",
     r"\((4-1)\times(3+1)=12\) parameters."),
    (r"Why does a positive coefficient in the B equation not guarantee that the probability of B rises?",
     r"Because the probability of B also depends on changes in the other categories; if another category's coefficient is larger, the probability of B can fall even though its ratio to the reference rises.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: multinomial logit from a contingency table",
                soal="Farmers' choice of marketing channel (A: middleman, reference; B: cooperative; C: direct market) by area. The numbers of farmers are in the table.",
                data=[["Area", "A", "B", "C", "Total"], ["Town (reference)", "30", "20", "10", "60"], ["Village", "20", "25", "25", "70"]],
                langkah=["Intercepts: log ratio of each category's probability to A in the reference area (town).",
                         "Village coefficient for B: the log odds ratio from the table.",
                         "Village coefficient for C.",
                         "Relative risk ratios."],
                simpul="Compared with town farmers, village farmers are 3.75 times more likely to sell directly at market (rather than to a middleman), and this difference is significant (z = 2.80). The tendency towards cooperatives is also higher (1.88 times) but not significant. Predicted probabilities for villages: A 0.29, B 0.36, C 0.36; for towns: 0.50, 0.33, 0.17."))

LE("regresi-logistik-ordinal",
   [("The proportional odds model and the latent variable",
     [r"A cumulative model models the probability that the response is in category \(j\) or lower, with different cut-points \(\alpha_j\) but a single set of coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\beta}\) shared by all cut-points. The model arises from a continuous latent variable cut into categories; the coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\beta}\) are the effects of the predictors on that latent variable.",
      r"With the parameterisation below, \(\beta>0\) means the predictor shifts the response towards higher categories, and \(e^{\beta}\) is the cumulative odds ratio of being above any cut-point, the same for every cut-point."]),
    ("Testing the proportional odds assumption",
     [r"The main assumption of the model is that a predictor's effect is the same at every cut-point. The score test or the Brant test compares the model with one allowing different coefficients per cut-point. A simple check: compute the observed cumulative odds ratio at each cut-point; if the values are similar, the assumption is reasonable.",
      r"If the assumption fails for some predictors, use a partial proportional odds model; if it fails badly, fall back to multinomial logit (losing the ordering information) or an adjacent-category model."]),
    ("Other ordinal models",
     [r"Besides the cumulative logit, there are the cumulative probit (normal latent variable), the complementary log-log (suited to rare high categories or discrete-time data), the adjacent-category model, which compares each category with the next, and the continuation-ratio model for staged processes such as levels of education."]),
    ("Why not use linear regression on the scores",
     [r"Coding the categories 1, 2, 3 and using linear regression assumes equal distances between categories and can give predictions outside the range. When there are few categories or the distribution is unbalanced (piled up at one end), the results are biased. Ordinal models use the ordering information without assuming distances."])],
   [(r"An ordinal model gives \(\beta=0.7\) for a treatment. Interpret it.",
     r"\(e^{0.7}=2.01\): the treatment doubles the odds of being above any cut-point (for example high score versus medium-low, and medium-high versus low)."),
    (r"With cut-points \(\alpha_1=-1\), \(\alpha_2=1\) and \(\mathbf{x}^\top\boldsymbol{\beta}=0.5\), compute the probabilities of the three categories.",
     r"\(P(y\le1)=1/(1+e^{1.5})=0.182\); \(P(y\le2)=1/(1+e^{-0.5})=0.622\). So 0.182, 0.440, 0.378."),
    (r"The response has 5 categories and there are 2 predictors. How many parameters does the proportional odds model have, and how many does multinomial logit have?",
     r"Proportional odds: 4 cut-points + 2 coefficients = 6. Multinomial: \(4\times3=12\)."),
    (r"The observed cumulative odds ratios are 4.0 at the first cut-point and 4.7 at the second. Is the proportional odds assumption reasonable?",
     r"Yes, the two values are fairly close; a difference of that size is expected from sampling variation. A formal test (Brant) can confirm it."),
    (r"Why must the cut-points \(\alpha_j\) be in increasing order?",
     r"Because the cumulative probability \(P(y\le j)\) must increase with \(j\); cut-points out of order would give negative category probabilities.")],
   contoh2=dict(judul="Second worked example: proportional odds from a 2 × 3 table",
                soal="Damage scores (1 light, 2 moderate, 3 severe) on control plants and pest-infested plants. The numbers of plants are in the table.",
                data=[["Group", "Light", "Moderate", "Severe", "Total"], ["Control (x = 0)", "20", "15", "5", "40"], ["Infested (x = 1)", "8", "16", "16", "40"]],
                langkah=["Check the assumption: observed cumulative odds ratios at both cut-points.",
                         "Maximum likelihood estimates of the proportional odds model.",
                         "The common cumulative odds ratio.",
                         "Predicted probabilities for the infested group."],
                simpul="Infested plants have 4.3 times the odds of being in a more severe damage category, at every cut-point. The observed odds ratios (4.0 and 4.7) support the proportional odds assumption."))
